+## Subtopics
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The loss involves matrix multiplications and traces, which can be sensitive to numerical precision. Normalization of normals is critical to avoid division by zero.
-*   **Boundaries:** The source mentions assuming outside faces are free, which may require special handling for boundary voxels.
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If the offset leads to degenerate triangles (zero area), the normal computation may fail. A small epsilon should be added to the norm calculation.
+### Asymptotic Decider
+Uses the trilinear interpolant's saddle point value to decide topology. Deterministic, not differentiable. [Link](790-asymptotic-decider.md)
+
+### Marching Cubes with Hermite Data and Full Case Table
+Uses gradient information (Hermite data) to resolve ambiguities. More complex than standard MC. [Link](961-marching-cubes-with-hermite-data-and-full-case-table.md)
+
+### Topologically Correct Marching Cubes
+Enforces topological correctness via discrete constraints. [Link](1368-topologically-correct-marching-cubes.md)
+
+### Topologically Defined Isosurfaces
+Defines isosurfaces based on topological properties. [Link](1428-topologically-defined-isosurfaces.md)
+
+### Topologically Defined Isosurface via Connectedness Couples
+Uses connectedness couples to ensure valid topology. [Link](1430-topologically-defined-isosurface-via-connectedness-couples.md)
+
+### Extended Trilinear Triangulation
+Extends the trilinear interpolation to resolve ambiguities. [Link](1618-extended-trilinear-triangulation.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The loss computation is dominated by the outer product and matrix multiplications, which are $O(N \cdot K^2)$ for $N$ grid cells and $K$ triangles. This is efficient on GPUs.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the connectivity matrix $W$ of size $N \times K \times K$.
-*   **Strengths:** Provides a differentiable signal for topological consistency, enabling end-to-end training of neural networks for surface extraction.
-*   **Limitations:** It is a soft constraint and does not guarantee topological correctness. It may converge to local minima where the surface is smooth but topologically incorrect.
-*   **When to Use:** When training learned Marching Cubes models where differentiability is required.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When strict topological correctness is required and differentiability is not needed; use deterministic methods like [Asymptotic Decider](790-asymptotic-decider.md) instead.
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot T^2)$ per cell, where $N$ is the number of cells and $T$ is the number of triangles. The outer product and matrix multiplication dominate.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot T^2)$ to store the connectivity matrix $W$.
+*   **Strengths**: Differentiable, allowing end-to-end training of neural networks for surface extraction. Encourages smooth surfaces by penalizing normal inconsistencies.
+*   **Limitations**: Computationally expensive due to the outer product and matrix operations. Requires careful tuning of the connectivity mask. May not guarantee topological correctness in all cases, as it is a soft constraint.
+*   **When to Use**: When training neural networks for implicit surface reconstruction or learned Marching Cubes.
+*   **When Not to Use**: When deterministic topological correctness is required, or when computational resources are limited.
 
 ## Background
-The Topology/Normal Consistency Loss was introduced in the context of deep learning for surface extraction, specifically in the "Deep Marching Cubes" framework [S1, unofficial]. It aims to bridge the gap between discrete topological constraints and differentiable optimization.
+The Topology/Normal Consistency Loss was introduced in the context of deep learning for 3D shape reconstruction, specifically in the "Deep Marching Cubes" framework [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to address the limitations of standard Marching Cubes when applied to neural network outputs, which may not satisfy the topological constraints required for valid triangulation. The method leverages the differentiability of the loss function to guide the network towards producing outputs that are topologically valid and geometrically smooth.
 
